Now we all have seen various messages about supercharacters in
the endgame boldy cutting apart groups of 100 archons with
cursed daggers in their hands without blinking an eye.
It is true, in fact, that when you have a character like this
everything after the castle becomes rather tedious, including
the endgame and such.
However, the direct cause behind such a misbalance lies
therein upon having the knowledge of all the tricks available
in the game.

Can you really blame nethack for being to easy if you know
all the tricks to win?  Now, I don't know about you, but
trying to learn all the suble tricks for fountains, cursing,
polymorphing, eating rings, etc by simply PLAYING the game
would take FOREVER.  And I guarantee you that you would hardly
be omnipotent.

Now I,  like many players, wanted to *win* the game, so I collected
hints etc. and read the spoilers available on this newsgroup and
in the spoiler file.  Yes, I DO know what many different ways
to get rid of a punishment scroll, I DO know the usefulness of
unicorn horns, I DO know this etc...

How did your superchar get that grey dragon scale mail? 
Did he kill a grey dragon and discover to read an enchant armor
while reading its scales? Or did he wish for it by simply guessing
the name of the armor?  That PYEC you got is pretty good, right?
It recharges everything (Except wishing wands) and even charges
ring bonuses.  You wonder if you know about the PYEC by actually
playing a Tourist and finishing the quest or by simply reading
this newsgroup and saying 'hey, that's a neat wish.'

Now, I know this sounds all a bit negative, but it is simply true.
True, the endgame and such is boring and perhaps we need some
higher, tougher monsters to challenge these characters, but
the plain fact of it is this: they exist ONLY because we,
experts at every facet of nethack, created them.  Sure, we
could make nethack an incredibly hard game, where even thos
who knew every trick in the book would still be vulnerable,
yet who's to blame for knowing all these tricks?
Yourself.

